{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786797095,"nonce":"LJMEm2MvHrE2VtJPX4thkM74bqk=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABM4Wz-A","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/103","origin":"github"},"title":"Trainable amplitude in a time/tau-dependent essential value (u(top) - g*tau)","message":"## What\n\nRuntime Dirichlet parameters landed (2026-08-15): a `jno.np.parameter` in an essential value trains on steady linear, steady nonlinear, and linear transient paths. The deliberately refused combination is a value that is **both** parametric and t/tau-dependent:\n\n```python\nu(*top)[1] - g * tau     # displacement control with a TRAINABLE ramp amplitude\n# NotImplementedError: ... BOTH runtime-parametric (['g']) and time/tau-dependent ...\n```\n\nThe refusal is correct today because the two held-value mechanisms cannot compose: the parametric branch would hold the value constant in tau (silently un-ramping the load), the temporal branch would freeze the parameter at its stored value (silently un-training it).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nDisplacement-controlled inverse problems: recover the grip amplitude of a softening test from the measured reaction curve — the natural companion to the tau-dependent essential value that drives the phase-field SENT study. Currently the workaround is training the amplitude through a Neumann/body term, which changes the physics of the control.\n\n## Sketch\n\nThe two mechanisms already share the held-value shape ((dofs, value_node, coords) stashes vs args-evaluated pairs). Composition = evaluating the value node with BOTH the temporal variable bound to the step time AND the parameters substituted from args — `_eval_value_node_at_time` and `_eval_value_node_at(params=)` merged into one evaluator call, on the paths that thread both t and args (the parametric residual `res_p(u, args, t)` already receives both).","files":null}]}